WEBVTT

1
00:00.550 --> 00:07.368
Welcome to Crosswalk, a behavioral intelligence panel observing the digital lives of over thirty million people worldwide.

2
00:07.368 --> 00:11.280
Right now, Sam in Oakland is checking the Golden State Warriors score.

3
00:11.280 --> 00:17.372
Quinn at the University of Michigan is putting off studying and watching a get ready with me video on TikTok.

4
00:17.372 --> 00:23.240
And Julie in Des Moines is adding things to her Amazon shopping cart that she will abandon by dinnertime.

5
00:23.240 --> 00:30.785
Not only do we see these digital actions, we also see every single action taken by our panelists across all devices, twenty-four seven.

6
00:30.785 --> 00:38.497
That is how we called Texas and Ohio before the polls closed, and put a network show's premiere within three percent of its real audience.

7
00:38.497 --> 00:42.130
While everyone else is guessing at human behavior, we observe it.

8
00:43.780 --> 00:50.097
Since January first, twenty twenty-one, over thirty million people worldwide have signed up for our platform.

9
00:50.097 --> 01:01.571
We've cut that down to a ten million, census-backed US gen pop panel, matched to gender, age, income, education level and location, so that we can confidently speak to how America behaves digitally.

10
01:01.571 --> 01:07.540
Our data is captured in real time and updated daily, so you're able to make key decisions in real time.

11
01:09.190 --> 01:11.771
Data provenance is incredibly important to us.

12
01:11.771 --> 01:18.054
We think everyone has a right to know what data is being collected from them, and a say in how it is being used.

13
01:18.054 --> 01:24.843
Our panelists choose to join our panel, install our software on their own devices, and agree to share their digital life.

14
01:24.843 --> 01:31.912
Identity is kept apart from behavior: personal details are removed and never shared, and each person becomes one anonymous ID.

15
01:31.912 --> 01:33.315
And they stay in control.

16
01:33.315 --> 01:38.420
They can opt out at any time, or change what information we capture and what we do with it.

17
01:40.070 --> 01:44.852
We then become a fly on the wall, observing how people live their digital lives.

18
01:44.852 --> 01:45.808
Meet Amy Samson.

19
01:45.808 --> 01:53.280
A twenty-eight year old who lives in Boulder, Colorado, works in aviation procurement, and has two dogs and a cat named Todd.

20
01:53.280 --> 01:57.703
Let's look at what Crosswalk sees as she moves throughout her digital day.

21
01:57.703 --> 02:00.452
At six forty-eight, Amy starts a Peloton ride.

22
02:00.452 --> 02:02.664
Then her morning playlist on Spotify.

23
02:02.664 --> 02:05.593
On her commute, she likes six posts on Instagram.

24
02:05.593 --> 02:08.462
At lunch she fills a Target cart and checks out.

25
02:08.462 --> 02:12.228
Mid-afternoon she reorders food for the dogs and Todd on Chewy.

26
02:12.228 --> 02:17.010
She looks at the same running shoes for the third time this week, and buys them.

27
02:17.010 --> 02:19.221
At six she orders dinner on DoorDash.

28
02:19.221 --> 02:23.405
At eight twenty she finishes the season finale of her show on Netflix.

29
02:23.405 --> 02:26.155
And before bed, twenty-five minutes of TikTok.

30
02:26.155 --> 02:34.583
Every click, in order, with the time of day, on every screen she owns: the tablet, the phone, the laptop, the connected TV, the game console.

31
02:34.583 --> 02:36.555
All of it under one anonymous ID.

32
02:36.555 --> 02:37.930
One person, one record.

33
02:39.580 --> 02:44.509
Our data is longitudinally rich, going back to January first, twenty twenty-one.

34
02:44.509 --> 02:47.097
The same people, recorded every day since.

35
02:47.097 --> 02:49.315
So we see the whole story, in order.

36
02:49.315 --> 02:52.458
We saw someone search where to watch Love Is Blind.

37
02:52.458 --> 02:53.628
Click a Netflix ad.

38
02:53.628 --> 02:54.799
Sign up that night.

39
02:54.799 --> 02:56.648
Binge every episode in a week.

40
02:56.648 --> 02:58.619
Cancel the day after the finale.

41
02:58.619 --> 03:04.596
And months later, open a Netflix email offering twenty-five percent off to rejoin, and come back.

42
03:04.596 --> 03:06.938
Every step, and the time between them.

43
03:06.938 --> 03:10.080
A billion new rows a week, refreshed every morning.

44
03:11.730 --> 03:19.391
The US panel is census matched: ten million people who look like the country, every age, income, ethnicity and state, in proportion.

45
03:19.391 --> 03:27.980
So when we count who did something, the count scales up to all three hundred twenty-nine point nine million Americans, in percentages and in people.

46
03:29.630 --> 03:31.877
Here's what you can do with the data.

47
03:31.877 --> 03:33.759
Profile IQ: who an audience is.

48
03:33.759 --> 03:40.499
Demographics, the brands they buy, what they watch, where they shop, hundreds of brands indexed against the US.

49
03:40.499 --> 03:51.430
Subscriber IQ: who subscribed to watch a title and what brought them there, how many finished it, who came back and who churned, and how much money the title made for the streamer.

50
03:51.430 --> 03:57.988
Digital Journey IQ: the path from the first search to the purchase, step by step, and where people drop off.

51
03:57.988 --> 04:04.667
Attribution IQ: which ad, post or placement led to the sale, across every touchpoint, not just the last click.

52
04:04.667 --> 04:08.857
Trends IQ: what people are searching, reading and watching this week.

53
04:08.857 --> 04:14.383
Rankers: every show, film, song, podcast and game ranked by what people watched and played.

54
04:14.383 --> 04:21.063
Micro-series IQ: the short vertical dramas, ranked by views, with who crosses the paywall and how many finish.

55
04:21.063 --> 04:27.499
Brand Tracking: a brand's paid accounts, retention and churn, month by month, against its competitive set.

56
04:27.499 --> 04:37.276
Brand Partnership IQ: what a talent and brand pairing was worth, in dollars: earned media, brand lift and conversion value, read against people who never saw it.

57
04:37.276 --> 04:38.308
Digital Twins IQ.

58
04:38.308 --> 04:46.020
Every audience gets its own room of twins: one seat for each archetype, each speaking for the millions of people it stands for.

59
04:46.020 --> 04:49.724
Ask them anything before you spend: a plan, a title, a price.

60
04:49.724 --> 04:51.789
And every call goes on the record.

61
04:51.789 --> 05:00.169
We called Texas and Ohio before the polls closed, and said NCIS: New York would draw ten point zero four million people in its first week.

62
05:00.169 --> 05:01.808
It drew ten point two five.

63
05:01.808 --> 05:03.083
Predictive Analytics.

64
05:03.083 --> 05:06.605
Ask one business question: if I do this, will that happen?

65
05:06.605 --> 05:16.382
We plan the room and run every question, from the big picture down to the decision, and hand you the call with the numbers behind it, in a ready-to-share report.

66
05:16.382 --> 05:27.008
It runs on the same tried and true models we use to help our Wall Street clients see what is coming before earnings, applied to ten million people and their years of behavior.

67
05:27.008 --> 05:29.377
And Prometheus, our research assistant.

68
05:29.377 --> 05:36.481
Ask it anything, in plain English, and it answers from what people did: who they are, what they buy, what they watch.

69
05:36.481 --> 05:41.400
In minutes, with the Excel file, the PowerPoint deck, or an email, ready to send.

70
05:41.400 --> 05:44.497
On the dashboard, on your desktop, and in your API.

71
05:44.497 --> 05:45.468
Twelve products.

72
05:45.468 --> 05:47.290
One set of ten million people.

73
05:48.940 --> 05:49.626
Who uses it.

74
05:49.626 --> 05:57.231
Studios and streamers, to answer how their content is performing, what made someone sign up for a platform, and what made them leave.

75
05:57.231 --> 06:04.321
Brands, to see who buys and who is about to, which talent moves them, why people abandon their cart, and where they go next.

76
06:04.321 --> 06:19.302
Agencies, to measure paid and organic content performance with multi-touch, coefficient-based attribution: a touchpoint that was not the last click still helped convert that customer, and gets its share of the credit, so you see the true value of your campaigns.

77
06:19.302 --> 06:28.107
Sports teams and leagues, to sell more tickets, to pitch, sell and value sponsorship packages, and to know what fans do when they are not in the building.

78
06:28.107 --> 06:40.515
Talent and influencer agencies, to understand a showrunner's, a celebrity's or a creator's audience, the value they bring to a network, a series or a brand partnership, and to negotiate richer deals for their clients.

79
06:40.515 --> 06:45.318
And finance desks and partners through our API, reading the same ten million people.

80
06:45.318 --> 06:48.120
Proven and adopted by the world's biggest brands.

81
06:49.770 --> 06:51.102
Why is this different?

82
06:51.102 --> 06:53.100
Surveys tell you what people say.

83
06:53.100 --> 06:54.493
Ratings see one screen.

84
06:54.493 --> 06:56.128
Web analytics see one site.

85
06:56.128 --> 06:57.823
Card data sees one checkout.

86
06:57.823 --> 07:10.176
Crosswalk sees the whole person across every screen, ten million times over, refreshed every morning, census matched to the country, and checked against real outcomes: elections, ratings, accounts opened.

87
07:10.176 --> 07:11.750
And it answers in minutes.

88
07:13.400 --> 07:14.835
And why can you trust it?

89
07:14.835 --> 07:18.278
Every person opted in, and can walk away whenever they like.

90
07:18.278 --> 07:22.353
Behavior is never tied to a name, and no individual is ever identified.

91
07:22.353 --> 07:25.911
Clients receive group statistics, never a file about a person.

92
07:25.911 --> 07:30.560
Zero-party data, with a provenance chain built for the toughest compliance teams.

93
07:32.210 --> 07:32.1000
Crosswalk.

94
07:32.1000 --> 07:34.501
Noise to knowledge.

95
07:34.501 --> 07:37.028
Stop guessing at human behavior.

96
07:37.028 --> 07:39.240
Sign up at crosswalknyc.com.
